{"pages":[" LANGCHAIN MASTERY The Complete LangChain Cheatsheet Every component, every concept, one real app built step-by-step. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET What is LangChain? WHAT IT IS LangChain is an open-source framework for building applications powered by LLMs. It doesn't replace the model — it gives you the plumbing around it: connecting prompts, models, data sources, memory, and tools into one coherent pipeline. THE CORE IDEA Instead of writing custom glue code every time you connect an LLM to a database, an API, or a document store, LangChain gives you standardized, reusable building blocks (called Runnables) that snap together. IN ONE LINE If the LLM is the engine, LangChain is the car — the chassis, wiring, and controls that turn raw model power into a working application. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Why Do We Need LangChain?  THE PROBLEM Raw LLM API calls are stateless, have no memory, can't access your private data, and can't take real-world actions on their own.  WHAT LANGCHAIN SOLVES Connects LLMs to your own documents/data (RAG) Adds memory across conversation turns Lets LLMs call external tools and APIs Standardizes prompt → model → output pipelines Makes multi-step, multi-model workflows composable  EXAMPLES A customer support bot that answers from your company docs (RAG) An agent that checks weather, books a flight, and emails confirmation (tools) A chatbot that remembers your name across 20 messages (memory) AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET LangChain Architecture Overview THE BIG PICTURE Every LangChain app is built from a combination of these core pieces: Models — the LLM or chat model Prompts — structured input templates Output Parsers — structured output extraction Runnables / LCEL — the composition layer connecting everything Memory — conversational state Document Loaders + Splitters — ingesting external data Embeddings + Vector Stores — semantic search Retrievers — fetching relevant context Chains — multi-step pipelines Agents + Tools — autonomous decision-making Callbacks — logging, monitoring, streaming We'll go through each one, then build a real app using them. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Models WHAT The interface to the actual LLM — LangChain supports two types: LLM (text-in, text-out) and ChatModel (message-in, message-out — what you'll use 95% of the time). KEY CLASSES ChatOpenAI, ChatAnthropic, ChatGoogleGenerativeAI, etc.  Code Explained: We instantiate ChatAnthropic as our model, implementing the standard Runnable interface. Swapping this for ChatOpenAI is a one-line change. from langchain_anthropic import ChatAnthropic model = ChatAnthropic(model=\"claude-sonnet-4-6\") response = model.invoke(\"Explain RAG in one line\") print(response.content)  WHY IT MATTERS: Swapping models is a one-line change — your prompts, chains, and logic stay identical regardless of provider. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Prompts WHAT Templates that structure input to the model — instead of hardcoding strings, you define reusable templates with variables. KEY CLASSES PromptTemplate (single string), ChatPromptTemplate (multi-message, with roles)  Code Explained: We define a template with placeholders like {role} and {question}. .invoke() fills these in before passing the prompt to the model. from langchain_core.prompts import ChatPromptTemplate prompt = ChatPromptTemplate.from_messages([ (\"system\", \"You are a helpful {role}.\"), (\"human\", \"{question}\") ]) formatted = prompt.invoke({\"role\": \"tutor\", \"question\": \"What is a vector?\"})  WHY IT MATTERS: Prompts become parameterized, testable, and reusable — not scattered string concatenation across your codebase. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Output Parsers WHAT Convert raw LLM text output into structured data your code can actually use — strings, JSON, lists, or custom objects. KEY CLASSES StrOutputParser, JsonOutputParser, PydanticOutputParser  Code Explained: We pipe prompt | model | parser. The parser takes raw text from the LLM and formats it for downstream code. from langchain_core.output_parsers import StrOutputParser parser = StrOutputParser() chain = prompt | model | parser result = chain.invoke({\"role\": \"tutor\", \"question\": \"What is a vector?\"})  WHY IT MATTERS: Without parsing, you're stuck doing fragile manual string manipulation on raw LLM output. Parsers make outputs reliable for downstream code. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Runnables & LCEL (the heart of LangChain) WHAT LCEL (LangChain Expression Language) is the | pipe syntax that chains components together. Every component — prompts, models, parsers, retrievers — implements the Runnable interface, meaning they all share the same .invoke(), .stream(), .batch() methods.  Code Explained: LCEL uses the | operator to chain components. Every part supports .invoke(), .stream(), and .batch() natively. chain = prompt | model | parser chain.invoke({\"role\": \"tutor\", \"question\": \"Explain embeddings\"}) chain.stream({\"role\": \"tutor\", \"question\": \"Explain embeddings\"}) # streaming chain.batch([{\"role\": \"tutor\", \"question\": \"Q1\"}, {\"role\": \"tutor\", \"question\": \"Q2\"}])  WHY IT MATTERS: This unified interface is what makes LangChain composable — any Runnable can be swapped, combined, or run in parallel using the same syntax. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Runnable Variants VARIANTS RunnableParallel — run multiple chains simultaneously, combine results RunnableLambda — wrap any custom Python function into the pipeline RunnablePassthrough — pass input through unchanged, often used to preserve original data alongside transformed data in RAG chains.  WHY IT MATTERS: These let you build non-linear pipelines — parallel branches, custom logic injection, and data preservation — all within the same LCEL syntax. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Memory WHAT Gives your chain awareness of previous conversation turns — without it, every .invoke() call is stateless and isolated. KEY APPROACHES Modern LangChain favors RunnableWithMessageHistory wrapping a chain, backed by a message store (in-memory, Redis, etc.)  Code Explained: We wrap our base chain with RunnableWithMessageHistory. It intercepts every input, fetches past messages, and appends them. from langchain_core.chat_history import InMemoryChatMessageHistory from langchain_core.runnables.history import RunnableWithMessageHistory store = {} def get_history(session_id): if session_id not in store: store[session_id] = InMemoryChatMessageHistory() return store[session_id] chain_with_memory = RunnableWithMessageHistory(chain, get_history)  WHY IT MATTERS: Without memory, your chatbot forgets the user's name the moment they say it. Memory makes multi-turn conversation possible. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Document Loaders WHAT Pull data from external sources (PDFs, websites, databases, Notion, etc.) into LangChain's standard Document format. KEY CLASSES PyPDFLoader, WebBaseLoader, CSVLoader, DirectoryLoader  Code Explained: PyPDFLoader takes a file path and converts it into Langchain Document objects, containing text and metadata. from langchain_community.document_loaders import PyPDFLoader loader = PyPDFLoader(\"company_policy.pdf\") docs = loader.load()  WHY IT MATTERS: This is step one of any RAG pipeline — without loaders, your LLM has no way to \"read\" your actual data sources in a standardized format. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Text Splitters WHAT Break large documents into smaller chunks that fit within context limits and retrieve cleanly — the chunking step we covered extensively in RAG. KEY CLASSES RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter (most common, splits intelligently by paragraph → sentence → word)  Code Explained: RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter slices documents into 500-character chunks with a 50-character overlap to preserve context. from langchain_text_splitters import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=500, chunk_overlap=50) chunks = splitter.split_documents(docs)  WHY IT MATTERS: Bad chunking breaks retrieval quality — this is the single most impactful preprocessing step in any RAG pipeline. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Embeddings WHAT Converts text chunks into numeric vectors capturing semantic meaning, used for similarity search. KEY CLASSES OpenAIEmbeddings, HuggingFaceEmbeddings, VoyageAIEmbeddings  Code Explained: OpenAIEmbeddings converts text chunks into high-dimensional vectors, which can be mathematically compared for semantic similarity. from langchain_openai import OpenAIEmbeddings embeddings = OpenAIEmbeddings(model=\"text-embedding-3-small\") vector = embeddings.embed_query(\"What is LangChain?\")  WHY IT MATTERS: Embeddings are what make semantic search possible — they're the foundation every vector store and retriever is built on. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Vector Stores WHAT Databases optimized to store embeddings and perform fast similarity search over them. KEY CLASSES Chroma, FAISS, Pinecone, Qdrant  Code Explained: We initialize a Chroma vector store directly from our document chunks and embedding model. from langchain_chroma import Chroma vectorstore = Chroma.from_documents(chunks, embedding=embeddings)  WHY IT MATTERS: This is where your processed, embedded knowledge actually lives — the searchable index your retriever queries against. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Retrievers WHAT The interface that takes a query and returns relevant documents from a vector store — wraps the vector store with extra retrieval logic.  Code Explained: vectorstore.as_retriever() creates a Runnable that, when queried, returns the top k most relevant chunks. retriever = vectorstore.as_retriever(search_kwargs={\"k\": 4}) relevant_docs = retriever.invoke(\"What is the refund policy?\") ADVANCED VARIANTS MultiQueryRetriever (query expansion), ContextualCompressionRetriever (reranking/compression)  WHY IT MATTERS: Retrievers are also Runnables — meaning they snap directly into LCEL chains alongside prompts and models. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Chains (Pre-built Patterns) WHAT Common multi-step LLM workflows, either built manually with LCEL or via LangChain's higher-level helper functions. KEY EXAMPLE RAG Chain  Code Explained: create_retrieval_chain combines a document-stuffing chain with our retriever to automate RAG. from langchain.chains.combine_documents import create_stuff_documents_chain from langchain.chains import create_retrieval_chain doc_chain = create_stuff_documents_chain(model, prompt) rag_chain = create_retrieval_chain(retriever, doc_chain) rag_chain.invoke({\"input\": \"What is the refund policy?\"})  WHY IT MATTERS: Chains save you from re-writing common patterns like \"retrieve, stuff into prompt, generate\" from scratch every time. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Tools WHAT Functions an LLM can call to interact with the outside world — APIs, calculators, search engines, databases.  Code Explained: We define get_weather and use the @tool decorator. LangChain inspects the signature to expose it to the LLM. from langchain_core.tools import tool @tool def get_weather(city: str) -> str: \"\"\"Get current weather for a city.\"\"\" return f\"It's sunny in {city}\"  WHY IT MATTERS: Tools are what turn a passive text generator into something that can take real actions — the foundation of agentic behavior. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Agents WHAT LLM-driven systems that decide which tools to call, in what order, based on reasoning — not a fixed predefined sequence.  Code Explained: We pass our model and tools into an AgentExecutor. The agent autonomously determines which tools to call. from langchain.agents import create_react_agent, AgentExecutor agent = create_react_agent(model, tools=[get_weather], prompt=agent_prompt) executor = AgentExecutor(agent=agent, tools=[get_weather]) executor.invoke({\"input\": \"What's the weather in Hyderabad?\"})  WHY IT MATTERS: Agents handle open-ended tasks where the steps aren't known in advance — the model reasons about what to do next at each step. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Callbacks WHAT Hooks into the execution lifecycle of a chain — used for logging, streaming tokens, monitoring costs, and debugging.  Code Explained: StdOutCallbackHandler hooks into the chain's execution to print intermediate steps, API calls, and raw outputs. from langchain_core.callbacks import StdOutCallbackHandler chain.invoke({\"question\": \"Explain RAG\"}, config={\"callbacks\": [StdOutCallbackHandler()]})  WHY IT MATTERS: In production, callbacks are how you get observability. By using the StdOutCallbackHandler, we can see the exact input and output at every stage of the chain. This is crucial for debugging complex agent workflows where the LLM might make multiple hidden API calls before returning a final answer to the user. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET LangGraph (Brief Mention) WHAT LangChain's framework for building more complex, stateful, cyclical agent workflows — when simple linear chains or basic agents aren't enough (e.g., multi-agent systems, loops, human-in-the-loop approval steps). WHEN TO USE Simple Q&A or RAG → stick with LCEL chains. Complex multi-step agent workflows with branching/looping logic → LangGraph.  WHY IT MATTERS: While standard LangChain LCEL is perfect for predictable, linear workflows, LangGraph introduces cycles and state. This allows you to build autonomous agents that can loop, self- correct, and pause for human approval before executing dangerous tools. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET BUILD: Project Overview WHAT WE'RE BUILDING A simple RAG-powered Q&A app over a company FAQ document — using nearly every component covered so far. FLOW Load document → Split into chunks → Embed → Store in vector DB → Retrieve relevant chunks → Generate grounded answer → Add memory for follow-up questions STACK LangChain + Anthropic Claude + Chroma vector store Let's build it step by step ➡️ AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Step 1: Load & Split the Document from langchain_community.document_loaders import TextLoader from langchain_text_splitters import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter loader = TextLoader(\"faq.txt\") docs = loader.load() splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=500, chunk_overlap=50) chunks = splitter.split_documents(docs) print(f\"Loaded {len(chunks)} chunks\")  WHAT'S HAPPENING: We load the raw FAQ file and break it into retrieval-friendly chunks. The 50-character overlap is critical: if a concept spans across the end of chunk A and the beginning of chunk B, the overlap ensures the LLM still gets the full context regardless of which chunk is retrieved. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Step 2: Embed & Store in Vector DB from langchain_openai import OpenAIEmbeddings from langchain_chroma import Chroma embeddings = OpenAIEmbeddings(model=\"text-embedding-3-small\") vectorstore = Chroma.from_documents(chunks, embedding=embeddings) retriever = vectorstore.as_retriever(search_kwargs={\"k\": 3})  WHAT'S HAPPENING: Each text chunk is converted into a high- dimensional vector using OpenAI's embedding model, and saved into a Chroma database. The retriever is configured with `k=3`, meaning it will only fetch the top 3 most semantically similar chunks when a user asks a question, saving tokens and improving accuracy. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Step 3: Build the Prompt & Model from langchain_anthropic import ChatAnthropic from langchain_core.prompts import ChatPromptTemplate model = ChatAnthropic(model=\"claude-sonnet-4-6\") prompt = ChatPromptTemplate.from_messages([ (\"system\", \"Answer only using the provided context. Context:\\n{context}\"), (\"human\", \"{input}\") ])  WHAT'S HAPPENING: We define our Claude 3.5 Sonnet model and a strict system prompt. Grounding the prompt with the `{context}` variable forces the LLM to strictly use the retrieved chunks to answer the user's question. This is the core mechanism that prevents the model from hallucinating. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Step 4: Assemble the RAG Chain from langchain.chains.combine_documents import create_stuff_documents_chain from langchain.chains import create_retrieval_chain doc_chain = create_stuff_documents_chain(model, prompt) rag_chain = create_retrieval_chain(retriever, doc_chain) response = rag_chain.invoke({\"input\": \"What is your refund policy?\"}) print(response[\"answer\"])  WHAT'S HAPPENING: The `create_stuff_documents_chain` stuffs the retrieved documents into the prompt, while the `create_retrieval_chain` handles the actual search against the vector database. This wires everything together so a single `.invoke()` triggers the entire pipeline seamlessly. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Step 5: Add Memory for Follow-Up Questions from langchain_core.chat_history import InMemoryChatMessageHistory from langchain_core.runnables.history import RunnableWithMessageHistory store = {} def get_history(session_id): if session_id not in store: store[session_id] = InMemoryChatMessageHistory() return store[session_id] conversational_rag = RunnableWithMessageHistory( rag_chain, get_history, input_messages_key=\"input\", history_messages_key=\"chat_history\" )  WHAT'S HAPPENING: We wrap our RAG chain in `RunnableWithMessageHistory`. This intercepts every incoming question, fetches the past chat history from a session-specific store, and appends it to the prompt. Now the LLM can resolve pronouns like \"it\" or \"that\" based on prior conversation turns. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Step 6: Run the Full App config = {\"configurable\": {\"session_id\": \"user_123\"}} r1 = conversational_rag.invoke({\"input\": \"What is your refund policy?\"}, config=config) print(r1[\"answer\"]) r2 = conversational_rag.invoke({\"input\": \"Does that apply to international orders too?\"}, config=config) print(r2[\"answer\"])  WHAT'S HAPPENING: Because we attached memory, the second question doesn't need to explicitly mention \"refund policy\". The chain understands it's a follow-up. This complete, production-ready pattern uses loaders, splitters, embeddings, vector stores, and memory all working in perfect harmony. AI Coach John Follow  Repost"," CHEATSHEET Quick Reference: Component Cheatsheet Model Talk to the LLM Prompt Structure input Output Parser Structure output Runnable/LCEL Compose everything Memory Persist conversation state Document Loader Ingest external data Text Splitter Chunk documents Embeddings Convert text → vectors Vector Store Store & search vectors Retriever Fetch relevant chunks Chain Multi-step pipeline Tool Action the LLM can call Agent Reasoning + tool use Callback Logging/monitoring LangGraph Complex stateful workflows AI Coach John Follow  Repost"]}